<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajax the Lesser</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Locrian Ajax</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ajax the Lesser is a hero of Greek mythology, son of Oileus and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. During the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> he dragged Cassandra from the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, a sacrilege for which the goddess wrecked him on his way home. The Locrians atoned for his crime by sending two noble maidens each year to serve in Athena's temple at Ilion, a custom attested into Roman times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajax-the-lesser.jpg" alt="Ajax the Lesser" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kodros Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attic_red-figure_cup_with_Ajax_and_Cassandra_Louvre_G_458.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ajax the Lesser, also called the Locrian Ajax, was a Greek mythological hero, called the lesser to tell him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajax-the-great">Ajax the Great</a>, son of Telamon. His mother was Eriopis, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> he was born at Naryx in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> he led his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> in forty ships. He is described as brave and skilled in throwing the spear, and next to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> the swiftest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. In the footrace at the funeral games of Patroclus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, who was hostile to him and favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, made him stumble. In later traditions he was a suitor of Helen, and in Etruscan legend he was called Aivas Vilates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, Ajax rushed into the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, where Cassandra had taken refuge embracing the goddess's statue, and dragged her away with the other captives. According to some writers he raped her inside the temple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> called for his death by stoning, but Ajax saved himself by swearing an oath of innocence to Athena while clutching her statue. According to the Bibliotheca, the seer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchas">Calchas</a> warned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> that Athena was furious and would destroy their ships unless they killed him. Ajax hid at the altar of another deity, and the Greeks, fearing divine retribution, let him live.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> to send a storm that sank many Greek ships. As Ajax sailed home, she struck his ship with a thunderbolt and wrecked it on the Whirling Rocks. He clung to a rock with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>'s help but then boasted that he would escape the sea against the will of the gods, and Poseidon split the rock with his trident and he was swallowed by the sea. Thetis buried him when his body washed up on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykonos">Mykonos</a>.</p><p>Other accounts place his death on the Capharean Rocks off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. His spirit was said to dwell on the island of Leuce. The Opuntian Locrians worshipped him as their national hero and, when they drew up for battle, left one place open for him, believing he fought among them unseen. His abduction of Cassandra was often represented in Greek art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ajax the Lesser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
